Teach FreeType's bundled `config.sub` about Apple's iOS-simulator host triplet.

`build.sh` configures FreeType with `--host=$HOST_TRIPLET --build=$BUILD_TRIPLET`
(the top-level configure delegates to `builds/unix/configure`, which is why the
patched file lives under `builds/unix/`). forge builds three iOS slices:
`arm64-apple-ios` (device), `arm64-apple-ios-simulator` and
`x86_64-apple-ios-simulator`. autoconf canonicalises the `--host` value through
`config.sub` before anything is compiled; for the simulator name that script
produces machine `<cpu>-apple` and OS `ios-simulator`, then splits the OS on
its dash into kernel `ios` and OS `simulator`. The bare word `simulator` gets
past the recognised-OS list only by accident -- it matches the unrelated `sim*`
entry meant for bare-metal simulator targets -- and then reaches the block that
validates the kernel/OS combination, which has no case for `ios` + `simulator`
and falls through to its catch-all:

    Invalid configuration 'arm64-apple-ios-simulator': Kernel 'ios' not known
    to work with OS 'simulator'.

configure exits there, so the two simulator slices fail with no compiler output
at all, while `arm64-apple-ios` and every Android triplet build normally.

FreeType 2.13.3 ships a recent config.sub (2024-05-27) in which the validating
switch carries a third "object file format" component, `case $kernel-$os-$obj
in`. The patch has three hunks, of which only the last one is load-bearing:

  1. `| ios*-simulator \` added to the `maybe_os` list of the three-component
     `*-*-*)` branch. This only affects the vendor-less spelling
     `<cpu>-ios-simulator`, which would otherwise be read as machine
     `<cpu>-ios` plus OS `simulator` (i.e. `ios` mistaken for the VENDOR
     field). forge always passes the four-component `<cpu>-apple-ios-simulator`,
     which takes the `*-*-*-*)` branch instead, so this hunk is not exercised
     by forge's own triplets -- it is defensive, and it does make
     `arm64-ios-simulator` parse correctly (`aarch64-unknown-ios-simulator`).

  2. `ios | ios-simulator)` added to the OS-normalisation switch. This one is a
     no-op: it is inserted immediately before that switch's `*)` catch-all,
     whose whole body is the comment "No normalization, but not necessarily
     accepted, that comes below" -- and by that point in the script the OS
     component of our triplet is the bare word `simulator` anyway, since the
     kernel/OS split has already happened. Deleting this hunk changes no
     output. Read it as documentation of intent rather than as behaviour.

  3. `ios-simulator-)` added to `case $kernel-$os-$obj in` -- kernel `ios`, OS
     `simulator`, empty `$obj` -- ahead of the `*-*-*)` catch-all that produces
     the error above. This is the hunk that actually fixes the build; with it
     alone, all three iOS slices canonicalise correctly. (flet-libtiff's
     equivalent patch spells the same case `ios-simulator*-` and inserts it a
     few lines further down; the two are interchangeable for forge's triplets.)

Without hunk 3, two of the three iOS slices of flet-libfreetype fail at
configure, and pillow -- which requires flet-libfreetype -- has no iOS wheels.
Android is unaffected.

The commit that introduced this file (ec8d184, a bulk branch consolidation)
records no rationale; the account above is reconstructed from the diff and
confirmed by running FreeType's own config.sub against forge's triplets with
each hunk applied and removed in turn.

diff --git a/builds/unix/config.sub b/builds/unix/config.sub
index 4aaae46..526f2d4 100755
--- a/builds/unix/config.sub
+++ b/builds/unix/config.sub
@@ -155,6 +155,7 @@ case $1 in
 			| storm-chaos* \
 			| uclinux-gnu* \
 			| uclinux-uclibc* \
+			| ios*-simulator \
 			| windows-* )
 				basic_machine=$field1
 				basic_os=$maybe_os
@@ -1727,6 +1728,8 @@ case $os in
 		obj=$os
 		os=
 		;;
+	ios | ios-simulator)
+		;;
 	*)
 		# No normalization, but not necessarily accepted, that comes below.
 		;;
@@ -2253,6 +2256,8 @@ case $kernel-$os-$obj in
 		# None (no kernel, i.e. freestanding / bare metal),
 		# can be paired with an machine code file format
 		;;
+	ios-simulator-)
+		;;
 	-*-)
 		# Blank kernel with real OS is always fine.
 		;;
